  "archived_eager_first_reader_rank_layer_chains": 2560,
  "clock_model": "Counterfactual: each receiving rank completes whole local experts in ascending physical index, charging one work unit per routed assignment. Compare f of each receiver’s own workload with f of the most loaded receiver’s workload, where all start together and have equal assignment rate. The latter allows shorter receivers to finish earlier. For each active DP separately, prioritize whole experts needed for its first 1, 2 or 3 local rows per active shard, or optimistically split expert work down to individual slots. Competing DPs cannot both claim first priority. Treat cross-card delivery as instant. A source row requires all six slots; a shard all three real rows; a TP group all active shards. The time sensitivity prices the busiest receiver at its entire measured F21 task duration, taking a median if tied. A 25% or 50% assignment-work share scales the early-release estimate proportionally. All other time fixed. Earlier readiness needs actual downstream overlap before it can improve the request.",
  "limits": [
    "Counters exclude inactive shards but preserve all experts serving other senders. Quantization branch, service occupancy, tiled completion within an expert, barriers and actual rank clock are not represented. The two graph layers do not provide ordinary or eager row readiness timings. Expert reordering needs matching dispatch/layout and synchronization; the slot-only option also splits expert batches and may increase GEMM, quantization and transport cost.",
    "The installed implementation synchronizes across ranks before unpermute. An earlier notification alone leaves the recorded consumer schedule unchanged when F21 exit, all other work and stream dependencies are fixed. An overlap needs a live consumer and row availability of both the shared and routed BF16 result, plus unchanged six-slot numerical order."
  ],
